  "stance": "bearish",
  "view": "Bitcoin fell 2.4% to $83,583 as $550 million in leveraged long positions were liquidated, a forced selling wave rather than a trend reversal. The move tracked a broader risk-off shift after Iranian attacks in the Strait of Hormuz pushed Brent crude above $101. A US government move of $103 million in seized Bitcoin to Coinbase Prime adds supply overhang, though no sale is confirmed. Near-term pressure likely persists.",
  "stories": [
    {
    "headline": "Bitcoin drops 2.4% as $550 million in leveraged bets liquidated",
    "essay": "Bitcoin fell as much as 2.4% to $83,583 on Wednesday, part of a broader crypto selloff that saw Ether drop 3.9% to $2,594 and smaller tokens like XRP and Solana fall 4.2% and 3.3%. The retreat accelerated as leveraged bets were unwound, with around $550 million in crypto positions liquidated over the last 24 hours, according to Coinglass data. Most of those liquidations came from long positions, said Dan Khus of LVRG Research, who called it a leverage flush rather than a downward trend.\n\nThe crypto losses mirrored a broader cooling in risk appetite. Renewed Iranian attacks in the Strait of Hormuz tempered hopes that shipping through the key waterway might return to pre-war levels, pushing Brent crude above $101 a barrel. US stock futures were little changed after the S&P 500 closed at an all-time high, while European stocks snapped a three-day winning streak and the 10-year Treasury yield climbed above 5.3%. Traders are watching the release of Federal Reserve meeting minutes later on Wednesday for clues on interest rates. A hawkish read could lift yields and the dollar, keeping pressure on risk assets, said Rachael Lucas of BTC Markets.\n\nThe slump comes after a recovery from Bitcoin's summer lows, though it remains well off its peak of $126,000 a year ago. Trading may stay choppy without a fresh catalyst. Pratik Kala of Apollo Crypto said that if Bitcoin loses $83,000, it could revisit the $78,000 region, which should provide some support.",
    "sources": [1]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Bitcoin cost bases show resistance at $88,000, support near $82,300",
    "essay": "Only one yearly Bitcoin buyer cohort remains underwater, according to checkonchain data. Investors who bought in 2025 have an average cost basis of approximately $88,000, which may now form a key resistance level due to sell pressure from relatively recent buyers. Bitcoin reached a monthly high of $87,500 in September before trading sideways and retreating below $84,000 as of Wednesday.\n\nThese yearly volume-weighted cost bases have acted as support and resistance throughout the cycle. As Bitcoin approaches a cohort's average purchase price, some investors may exit at breakeven while others add to positions. Bitcoin climbed to about $82,100 in May, matching the 2024 cohort's average cost basis, before hitting resistance and falling back to $60,000. It eventually broke above that level in August. The 2023 cohort's cost basis, around $65,000, has provided recurring support. When Bitcoin fell towards $60,000 in February, that cohort's cost basis was also near that level and broadly held as support throughout the 2026 bear market, though Bitcoin briefly traded below it. The 2026 cohort has an average cost basis of approximately $73,500, and those buyers have largely been in profit since late August when Bitcoin rallied above that threshold.\n\nAnother level to watch is the US spot Bitcoin ETF cost basis, which measures the average cost of deposits into the funds and currently stands at approximately $82,300. ETF investors only recently returned to profit for the first time this year, making this a potential support level if Bitcoin falls further.",
    "sources": [2]
    },
    {
    "headline": "US government moves $103 million in seized Bitcoin and BNB",
    "essay": "Wallets labeled as belonging to the US government moved about $103.2 million in Bitcoin and BNB on Tuesday, according to blockchain analytics firm Arkham. The Bitcoin portion was 833.6 BTC, worth about $71.6 million, which hopped through two unlabeled addresses before reaching deposit addresses tagged as Coinbase Prime. The rest was 40,285 BNB, worth $31.63 million, sent to an unlabeled address and then passed to another.\n\nArkham ties about 568.7 BTC to forfeited funds from Sergei Potapenko and Ivan Turõgin, who ran the crypto-mining service HashFlare. Prosecutors say HashFlare took in more than $577 million in sales between 2015 and 2019. The other 264.9 BTC came from the Bitfinex hack. The BNB is traced to funds seized from Alameda Research. No sale has been announced. Coinbase Prime also acts as the government's custodian, so a deposit can mean storage, as in a similar $288 million move in July. The government hasn't said which this is.\n\nThe US government holds about 325,000 BTC, worth roughly $27 billion, or about 1.6% of Bitcoin's supply, according to Arkham's September research. That makes it the largest government holder in Arkham's ranking, ahead of the UK at 61,245 BTC and El Salvador at 7,200 BTC. When the government sent 19,800 BTC from the Silk Road haul to Coinbase in December 2024, worth about $1.92 billion, Bitcoin slid more than 2% within 24 hours. Traders watch these wallets because a large sale can push prices down. A March 2025 executive order created a Strategic Bitcoin Reserve of forfeited Bitcoin that shall not be sold, but coins tied to open criminal cases sit outside it.",
    "sources": [6]
    }
  ]
}

