    "essay": "Bitcoin fell as much as 2.4% to $83,583 on Wednesday, part of a broader crypto selloff that saw Ether drop 3.9% to $2,594 and smaller tokens like XRP and Solana fall 4.2% and 3.3%. The retreat accelerated as leveraged bets were unwound, with around $550 million in crypto positions liquidated over the last 24 hours, according to Coinglass data. Most of those liquidations came from long positions, said Dan Khus, chief analyst at LVRG Research, who called the dip a leverage flush rather than a downward trend.\n\nThe crypto losses reflected a broader cooling in risk appetite. Renewed Iranian attacks in the Strait of Hormuz tempered hopes that shipping through the key waterway may return to pre-war levels, pushing Brent crude above $101 a barrel. US stocks futures were little changed after the S&P 500 closed at an all-time high in the prior session, while Europe's Stoxx 600 snapped three straight days of gains and the 10-year Treasury yield climbed above 5.3%.\n\nTraders are watching the release of Federal Reserve meeting minutes later on Wednesday for clues on interest rates. Rachael Lucas, an analyst at BTC Markets, said a hawkish read could lift yields and the US dollar, keeping pressure on risk assets. The slump comes after a recovery from Bitcoin's summer lows, though it remains well off its peak of $126,000 a year ago. Pratik Kala, a portfolio manager at Apollo Crypto, said if Bitcoin loses $83,000 it is possible to revisit the $78,000 region, which should provide some support.",
