    {
    "headline": "Metaplanet sells 10,000 Bitcoin, buys back 11,000 in liquidity demonstration",
    "essay": "Metaplanet disclosed that during the third quarter it sold 10,000 Bitcoin for approximately ¥124.7 billion, or around $790 million, and subsequently purchased 11,000 Bitcoin for roughly ¥149.9 billion, equivalent to around $950 million. The result was a net increase of 1,000 Bitcoin, leaving the company holding 44,000 Bitcoin at the end of the quarter.\n\nThe company described the operation as a demonstration of liquidity. It said proceeds from the 10,000 Bitcoin sale exceeded the outstanding principal of its bonds, borrowings and other interest-bearing liabilities, showing that its Bitcoin position could cover debt obligations if necessary. That matters because the corporate Bitcoin model inevitably creates questions about what happens when a company needs cash. Creditors and rating agencies care about whether assets can be mobilized in practice without disrupting the broader capital structure.\n\nThe disclosure arrived alongside a broader change in capital allocation policy. Metaplanet is preparing to put roughly 10% to 15% of its assets into strategic investments outside Bitcoin, including income-generating opportunities. That does not mean the company is abandoning its Bitcoin treasury strategy. Selling 10,000 Bitcoin and ending up with 11,000 back on the books suggests the company wanted to prove flexibility without reducing its overall exposure.",
