    "headline": "Gold slides as Fed minutes keep December hike in play",
    "essay": "Gold sold off Wednesday after the Federal Reserve's September meeting minutes showed most officials still expect another rate hike by year-end. Spot gold traded near $4,109.90 an ounce, down 1.28% on the session, while spot silver fell 2.55% to near $59.67. December futures had opened at $4,195 and slid to $4,138.10 by 6:41 a.m. ET.\n\nThe pressure came from rates and the dollar. The 10-year Treasury yield reached 5.36% before easing to around 5.28% after a strong $39 billion 10-year note auction, and the dollar index firmed. Higher yields raise the opportunity cost of holding gold, which pays no income. Markets still price about an 85% chance of a December hike, even as October odds fell to roughly 19% to 21% after September payrolls rose just 29,000 and July and August were revised down by a combined 60,000.\n\nTechnically, spot gold sits below a descending trendline from mid-August highs. A break below $4,104, the two-month low, opens the $4,000 area and then the year-to-date low at $3,941. Resistance runs from $4,118 to $4,151, with $4,200 needed to shift the bias. For a holder, the near-term path of least resistance is lower until yields or the dollar turn.",
