    "headline": "China buys 21 tonnes of gold in September, largest in three years",
    "essay": "The People's Bank of China added 21 tonnes of gold in September, lifting reserves to 2,196 tonnes and marking a 23rd straight month of purchases. It was the largest monthly increase since September 2023, when reserves rose 24 tonnes, and topped August's 18.5 tonnes and July's 18 tonnes.\n\nThe buying fits a broader pattern. Global central banks reported net purchases of 39 tonnes in August, taking the 2026 total to 170 tonnes through August, according to the World Gold Council. Poland leads year-to-date buying at 98 tonnes and holds 648 tonnes, closing in on a 700-tonne target, with China second at 80 tonnes. China's foreign exchange reserves fell $38.1 billion, or 1.11%, to $3.4 trillion at end-September on currency translation and asset price moves.\n\nThis official-sector demand has continued through a volatile stretch for gold, including an 8% drop in September, and gives the market a structural floor even as futures traders cut positions. It does not by itself reverse the current downtrend, but it limits how far prices can fall.",
