    {
    "headline": "Hormuz tanker attacks keep oil and inflation risk alive",
    "essay": "At least 12 attacks on oil, LNG and LPG tankers around the Strait of Hormuz were tracked during the week of Sept. 28 to Oct. 5, the highest weekly total since the Iran war began, and a Panama-flagged tanker was hit in the waterway. The U.S.-Iran situation remains unresolved.\n\nOil still settled lower, with Brent at $100.20 a barrel, down 0.4%, and WTI at $88.28, down 1.3%, as traders focused on recovering Middle East flows, emergency stock releases and expectations that about 90% of Hormuz flows are still moving. Lower crude reduces the immediate inflation impulse behind yields and the dollar, which is a mild positive for gold.\n\nThe risk cuts both ways. Persistent attacks, higher insurance costs and Gulf routing risk keep a defensive bid under bullion, but a fresh escalation that pushes oil higher would feed inflation expectations and strengthen the case for more Fed tightening. For now the oil market has not delivered the inflation shock that would change the rate outlook.",
