  "essay": "GE Aerospace shipped four F404-IN20 engines to HAL in September 2026, taking total supplies under the current contract to 14. Three engines went in August. The F404-IN20 is the engine for the Tejas Mk-1A, producing up to 85 kN with afterburner.\n\nThe contract covers 99 engines worth ₹5,375 crore (about $716 million), signed in 2021 alongside the first order for 83 Tejas Mk-1A jets. HAL has not delivered a single Mk-1A to the IAF. At least 25 airframes are ready and waiting for engines. The first aircraft was due in March 2024; that deadline was missed, partly because GE could not supply engines on time. GE had closed the F404 production line for lack of orders and needed about five years to restart it and rebuild its supply chain. HAL has imposed contractual penalties on GE for late supplies.\n\nDefence secretary Rajesh Kumar Singh said HAL would receive four engines a month from October to December, which would take the total to 22 by year-end. He said HAL could deliver a squadron of Mk-1A jets in late 2026 or early 2027 if radar and software integration issues are resolved. GE is expected to deliver more than 20 engines annually from 2027. HAL's production lines in Bengaluru and Nashik can build 24 aircraft a year. The IAF has ordered 180 Mk-1A jets across two contracts worth ₹1.1 lakh crore, and HAL signed a follow-on agreement in November 2025 for 113 more engines. Engine arrivals remain the bottleneck for converting airframes into deliveries.",
