},
{
  "headline": "HAL to lift AL-31FP engine output to 50 units a year",
  "essay": "HAL will raise annual production of AL-31FP engines, which power the IAF's Su-30MKI fleet, from 30 to 50 units with a new investment of ₹335 crore. The announcement came as HAL's Koraput division delivered its 2,000th aero-engine since inception.\n\nThe expansion supports a ₹26,000 crore contract signed with the Ministry of Defence in September 2024 for 240 AL-31FP engines. HAL has supplied 63 engines so far, beating its initial target of 60 by the end of the contract's second year. Koraput has overhauled more than 9,000 engines and has built powerplants for the MiG-21, MiG-27, MiG-29 and Su-30MKI.\n\nSeparately, Defence Minister Rajnath Singh announced a ₹218 crore investment for a second production line for Shakti helicopter engines at Koraput. That line should be operational by 2028-29 and produce up to 100 engines a year for the Dhruv and Prachand helicopters. Singh also flagged a proposed ₹5,000 crore indigenous high-thrust engine programme, including India's first high-thrust engine testing facility, and a partnership with Mishra Dhatu Nigam to indigenise raw materials. The DRDO's Kaveri engine could not meet the Tejas thrust requirement, which is why the jet depends on GE's F404. A DRDO proposal to co-develop a 120kN-class engine with France's Safran for the AMCA is awaiting clearance from the Cabinet Committee on Security.",
  "sources": [6]
},
{
  "headline": "HAL takes full control of HATSOFF helicopter training unit",
  "essay": "HAL has agreed to acquire the remaining 50% equity stake in HATSOFF Helicopter Training Pvt. Ltd. from CAE, Canada, at nil consideration. The deal converts the joint venture into a wholly owned HAL subsidiary.\n\nThe transaction covers 3,84,04,205 equity shares with a face value of ₹10 each. Completion is expected within 60 days of signing the Share Transfer Agreement. HAL already held 50%, so HATSOFF was a related party under SEBI rules, and the filing states the deal is not at arm's length. HAL has administrative approval from the Ministry of Defence and concurrence from DIPAM in the Finance Ministry.\n\nHATSOFF provides simulator-based training for military and civil helicopter pilots. Its customers include the Indian Air Force, Army, Navy, Coast Guard and BSF, plus civil operators Pawan Hans and Global Vectra. It reported turnover of ₹80.95 crore in FY26, up from the previous year, with paid-up capital of ₹76.80 crore and authorised capital of ₹76.84 crore. HAL paid nothing for CAE's half, which suggests the exit was settled through mechanisms outside this cash agreement or that the book value was judged negligible against the strategic control gained. HAL says full ownership will streamline decision-making in the helicopter training segment.",
