      "essay": "HAL has agreed to acquire the remaining 50% equity in HATSOFF Helicopter Training Pvt. Ltd. from CAE, Canada, at nil consideration. The deal converts the joint venture into a wholly owned subsidiary. It covers 3,84,04,205 equity shares with a face value of ₹10 each, and completion is expected within 60 days of signing the Share Transfer Agreement.\n\nHATSOFF runs simulator-based training for military and civil helicopter pilots, serving the Indian Air Force, Army, Navy, Coast Guard and BSF, plus civil operators like Pawan Hans Limited and Global Vectra. Its FY26 turnover was ₹80.95 crore, up from the previous year, with paid-up capital of ₹76.80 crore. HAL already held 50%, so the entity was a related party under SEBI rules, and the filing notes the transaction is not at arm's length. HAL has administrative approval from the Ministry of Defence and concurrence from DIPAM.\n\nFull ownership gives HAL direct control of the training segment and simplifies decision-making there. The nil consideration means no cash outflow for the stake, though the filing gives no cash value for what HAL gains.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "HAL has still delivered no Tejas Mk-1A jets to the IAF",
      "essay": "HAL has not delivered a single LCA Tejas Mk-1A fighter to the Indian Air Force so far. The first aircraft, ordered in February 2021, was due in March 2024. That deadline was missed because of engine delays and slow key certifications. The IAF chief, Air Chief Marshal A.P. Singh, said last week that the delay is hurting the air force as its fighter squadron strength falls. He said the country needs to produce up to 40 aircraft a year to plug capability gaps.\n\nThe order book is large. The IAF has placed two contracts for 180 Mk-1A jets worth a combined ₹1.1 lakh crore: 83 aircraft signed in February 2021, and 97 more in September 2025. HAL says deliveries under the first contract are likely to begin in December. The former HAL chairman, D K Sunil, said in April that GE had committed to supplying 20 engines between June and December 2026. The Mk-1A replaces the MiG-21s, which retired last year after more than six decades.\n\nFor HAL, the engine flow is the gating factor on turning a ₹1.1 lakh crore order book into revenue. Deliveries under the second contract are expected to begin in 2027-28.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Jio Platforms|https://m.economicti.text:
