  "view": "The Jio Platforms IPO is now firmly dated: open October 21, close October 23, list around October 28, with the RHP expected October 12. The reported raise is unsettled, $3.8 billion (Rs 37,700 crore) from Reuters versus Rs 31,000-32,000 crore elsewhere, and no final price band exists. Akash Ambani calls it a great responsibility and the start of a journey, while staying quiet on tariffs after Airtel's Rs 50 postpaid hike. Mixed, so neutral.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Jio Platforms IPO set for October 21 open, October 28 listing",
      "essay": "Jio Platforms plans to open its IPO on October 21 and close on October 23, with shares listing around October 28, according to reported timelines. The anchor book is expected October 19, and the RHP is likely to be filed around October 12. The company filed its DRHP with SEBI on June 19, 2026.\n\nThe issue is a fresh sale of up to 27 crore equity shares of face value Rs 10 each, with no offer-for-sale component, so the money goes to Jio Platforms rather than selling shareholders. Up to Rs 27,500 crore is earmarked to prepay or repay borrowings of Reliance Jio Infocomm, its material subsidiary, with the rest for general corporate purposes. Reliance Industries held 66.43 per cent before the issue; Meta affiliate Jaadhu Holdings held 9.98 per cent and Google International 7.73 per cent.\n\nFor FY26, Jio Platforms reported revenue from operations of Rs 1.47 lakh crore and profit after tax of Rs 30,049 crore. Reliance Jio served 524.4 million customers as of March 31, 2026. The reported size is unsettled: Reuters reported about $3.8 billion, roughly Rs 37,700 crore, while other reports say Rs 31,000-32,000 crore. No final price band is in the DRHP, and the RHP will settle the numbers.",
      "sources": [2, 4, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Akash Ambani calls Jio IPO a great responsibility",
      "essay": "Akash Ambani, Reliance Jio Chairman, said the Jio Platforms IPO is a great responsibility toward shareholders and the start of Jio's next growth phase. Speaking at India Mobile Congress in New Delhi on October 8, he noted it is the first public issue from the Reliance group in nearly three decades. He said he is hopeful the offering will be India's biggest IPO.\n\nOn tariffs, Ambani did not follow Bharti Airtel, which raised its postpaid plan by Rs 50 across the board this week. Past precedent suggests other operators follow when one raises tariffs, but Jio and Vodafone Idea have announced nothing. Ambani said Jio will keep giving disruptive value and stay the most affordable for consumers, and wants to democratise technology to the last level of India.\n\nJio's subscriber base has crossed the 50 crore mark. Brokerage Motilal Oswal noted the offering could re-rate not just Reliance Industries but Bharti Airtel as well. For anyone holding Jio Platforms, the listing is the event; the tariff decision is the near-term earnings lever still unspoken.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reliance may be a cheaper route to Jio after listing",
      "essay": "Reliance Industries shares imply a roughly 36 per cent discount on its two-thirds stake in Jio Platforms, according to Nimish Maheshwari of Beat The Street, who sees about 25 per cent as sustainable, in line with how the market values Bharti Airtel's holdings in its listed subsidiaries. Such holding-company discounts are common because parent shareholders get only indirect exposure.\n\nJio may seek a valuation of about Rs 11 lakh crore ($114 billion) in the IPO, Bloomberg reported. That would value Reliance's stake at about Rs 7.3 lakh crore, roughly 45 per cent of the parent's current market value. Brokerages including Motilal Oswal, Yes Securities and Nuvama value the stake at Rs 331 to 450 per Reliance share, or about 27 per cent to 37 per cent of the parent's share price.\n\nReliance shares have fallen 23 per cent this year, against a 14 per cent decline in the Nifty 50, on higher oil prices, elevated global bond yields and a weaker rupee. Maheshwari said the key trigger is transparent price discovery rather than immediate monetisation. A higher public-market valuation for Jio would raise the visible value of Reliance's holding and could narrow the discount. Thea Jamison of Change Global Investment said how Reliance monetises its Jio stake later could be another source of value.",
