      "essay": "Flipkart's Big Billion Days sale starts at midnight, with early access on October 8 and the general sale on October 9. Smartphone deals lead the push. The iPhone 17 256GB is listed at Rs 84,999, and with a Rs 2,000 bank discount and a Rs 3,000 exchange bonus the effective price can fall to Rs 79,999, against a launch price of Rs 99,900. Flipkart is offering a 10% discount with Axis and ICICI Bank cards.\n\nOther offers: the Poco X8 Pro Max 5G starts at Rs 42,999, the Mivi One 5G from Rs 11,999, the Realme P4 5G at Rs 29,999, the Moto Edge 70 Fusion from Rs 29,999, and the Vivo T5x from Rs 24,999. Amazon's Great Indian Festival runs at the same time, with the iPhone 16 at Rs 71,999 and the Samsung Galaxy S25 Ultra at Rs 84,999, down from a Rs 1,29,999 launch price.\n\nFlipkart is also running a campaign, 'Home for Old Age Appliances', built around its appliance exchange offer and an exchange bonus during the sale. The pitch targets customers who repair old appliances instead of replacing them. For the topic, the sale is the near-term volume driver, and the discounting is heavy.",
      "sources": [1, 4, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Minutes ranks second in quick-commerce availability",
      "essay": "Flipkart Minutes ranked second in product availability among India's major quick-commerce platforms, at 88.7%, according to a Bernstein report. Blinkit led at 94.4%, ahead of Swiggy Instamart at 83.8% and Zepto at 82.5%. Bernstein tracked 150 stock-keeping units across grocery, fresh and non-grocery categories over 10 days, seven time slots a day, and at least three PIN codes each in Mumbai, Gurugram and Bengaluru.\n\nAvailability held steadier at Blinkit and Flipkart Minutes, which each saw a 5% decline as the day progressed. Instamart fell 11% and Zepto 6%. Bernstein tied Instamart's drop to demand forecasting and inventory replenishment problems. It called product availability a key differentiator in a market that has competed on delivery speed and pricing.\n\nFlipkart also hired Dibyendu Sen as associate director of brand partnerships and monetisation for Flipkart Minutes, starting September 2026. He spent two years at Criteo driving retail media monetisation across Blinkit, Swiggy Instamart and Zepto, and previously worked at Flipkart from 2022 to 2024. The hire points to advertising revenue as the next lever for Minutes.",
      "sources": [3, 5]
    }
  ]
}

