{
  "stance": "bearish",
  "view": "Bitcoin is under pressure: it trades near $83,000, down about 34% from its all-time high, and sits just $300 above a level that could trigger a drop to $77,000. ETF inflows have collapsed from $342 million to $35 million as the 10-year Treasury yield hit 5.27%, cutting institutional demand. A $64 billion supply wall between $84,000 and $84,500 caps upside. The UK's proposed HMRC powers add a privacy overhang, but the immediate driver is weak flows and technical fragility.",
  "stories": [
    {
    "headline": "Bitcoin tests key support as ETF inflows dry up",
    "essay": "Bitcoin is trading around $83,000, down nearly 3% on October 7 and 1.5% in the last 24 hours. It sits just $300 above the $82,700 trigger that Jesse Marre of the Hilbert Group says could send it to $77,000. A move to $98,000 would require a 5% climb and a break above $87,400, the top of its recent range.\n\nThe immediate drag is institutional demand. Bitcoin ETF daily inflows have collapsed from $342 million to $35 million as the 10-year Treasury yield hit 5.27%, making bonds more competitive. On-chain data from Bitfinex Alpha shows $64 billion in Bitcoin clustered between $84,000 and $84,500, a supply wall that buyers must clear to push higher.\n\nThe price is also 34.2% below its all-time high of $126,080 and roughly flat for 2026. For holders, the setup is fragile: a break below $82,700 likely opens $77,000, while a sustained move above $87,400 is needed to revive the case for $94,000 to $98,000.",
