topic|Jio Platforms|https://www.tradingv.text:
{
  "stance": "bullish",
  "view": "The IPO is now concrete: opening around October 21, listing around October 28, raising about ₹37,700 crore, with up to ₹27,500 crore going to repay Reliance Jio Infocomm debt. Jio's FY26 revenue of ₹1.47 lakh crore and profit of ₹30,049 crore back the scale. The open question is the holding-company discount on Reliance, which analysts put at roughly 36 per cent versus a sustainable 25 per cent.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Jio Platforms IPO set for October 21, India's largest ever",
      "essay": "Jio Platforms plans to open its IPO on October 21 and list around October 28, raising about ₹37,700 crore, according to Reuters. That would make it India's biggest-ever IPO, ahead of Hyundai Motor India's ₹27,870 crore issue in 2024 and the National Stock Exchange's roughly ₹22,500 crore offering earlier this year. The dates are reported, not final, until the company files its offer document.\n\nThe issue is a fresh sale of up to 27 crore equity shares of face value ₹10 each, with no offer-for-sale component, so the money goes to Jio Platforms rather than existing shareholders. Up to ₹27,500 crore is earmarked to prepay or repay borrowings of Reliance Jio Infocomm, its material subsidiary, with the rest for general corporate purposes. SEBI cleared the issue with an observation letter in August.\n\nThe company reported FY26 revenue from operations of ₹1.47 lakh crore and profit after tax of ₹30,049 crore, with EBITDA of ₹76,255 crore. Reliance Industries holds 66.43 per cent, Meta affiliate Jaadhu Holdings 9.98 per cent and Google International 7.73 per cent. For anyone holding the topic, the size and the debt repayment plan are the two facts that matter most.",
      "sources": [2, 4, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Akash Ambani calls IPO a responsibility, keeps affordability pledge",
      "essay": "Akash Ambani, chairman of Reliance Jio, described the IPO as a great responsibility to shareholders and said the listing starts Jio's next phase of growth. Speaking to CNBC-TV18 at India Mobile Congress in New Delhi on Thursday, October 8, he said the group has a firm philosophy on shareholder value and noted this is the first public issue from the Reliance group in nearly three decades.\n\nOn tariffs, Ambani said Jio will keep giving disruptive value and stay the most affordable option for consumers. His comments came after Bharti Airtel raised its postpaid plan tariff by ₹50 across the board. Past precedent suggests other operators follow when one raises tariffs, but neither Jio nor Vodafone Idea has announced anything yet.\n\nJio had 508.43 million mobile subscribers at the end of August, a 39.31 per cent market share, and added 2.40 million during the month, the most of any operator. The subscriber base has crossed the 50 crore mark. Ambani's affordability line matters because it signals Jio will not chase tariff hikes in the near term, which caps near-term revenue per user upside for the sector.",
      "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reliance trades at discount to its Jio stake before listing",
      "essay": "Reliance Industries shares imply a roughly 36 per cent discount on its two-thirds stake in Jio Platforms, according to Nimish Maheshwari of research firm Beat The Street. He sees about 25 per cent as sustainable, in line with how the market values Bharti Airtel's holdings in its listed subsidiaries. Such discounts are common for parent companies because shareholders only get indirect exposure to the businesses they own.\n\nJio may seek a valuation of about ₹11 trillion ($114 billion) in the IPO, Bloomberg reported. That would value Reliance's stake at about ₹7.3 trillion, or about 45 per cent of the parent's current market value. Brokerages including Motilal Oswal, Yes Securities and Nuvama value the stake at ₹331 to ₹450 per Reliance share, or around 27 per cent to 37 per cent of the parent's share price.\n\nReliance shares have fallen 23 per cent this year, against a 14 per cent decline in the Nifty 50, as higher oil prices, elevated global bond yields and a weaker rupee weigh on Indian equities. Thea Jamison of Change Global Investment said there is no strong justification for a discount as steep as 50 per cent, and how Reliance monetises its Jio stake later could add value. For holders, the trigger is transparent price discovery after listing, not immediate monetisation.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

