  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Fed signals more hikes, keeping gold under pressure",
      "essay": "Gold edged up to around $4,140 in early Asian trading, rebounding from a two-month low, but the Fed's hawkish tone caps gains. Minutes from the last meeting showed policymakers united behind September's hike, with most judging another increase appropriate by year-end. Governor Waller said more hikes are likely needed, though he left room for a pause in October. Traders price a 17.7% chance of an October hike and an 81.3% chance of a December move, per CME FedWatch. Higher rates raise the opportunity cost of holding gold, which pays no yield.\n\nTechnically, gold trades below its 100-day simple moving average near $4,265 and the Bollinger middle band around $4,240, with the RSI at 39.99, below neutral. Support sits near $4,060. Analysts at Scotiabank said gold looks vulnerable after breaking under $4,100, while copper holds near record highs. For gold holders, the Fed path remains the main headwind.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trump rules out Iran strike before midterms, easing haven demand",
      "essay": "President Trump said the US won't attack Iran before the November midterm elections, adding that Washington is having productive discussions with Iran. The remarks followed a report that the White House was considering striking Iran ahead of the midterms. The shift reduces immediate safe-haven demand for gold, which often rises on geopolitical tension. Gold was trading near $4,117 an ounce as of 9 a.m. Eastern, up $99 from yesterday but only $82 above a year ago.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Central banks keep buying gold, led by China",
      "essay": "China's central bank added about 23 tonnes of gold in September, its 23rd straight month of buying. Central banks worldwide bought a net 39 tonnes in August, according to the World Gold Council. Russia's central bank has sold 56 tonnes this year, which Peter Schiff said is what reserves are for when a country at war needs cash. Schiff also said rising bond yields are bullish for gold because bond investors losing money will move into gold. The 10-year Treasury yield touched 5.35%, its highest since 2002. For gold holders, steady official-sector demand offers a floor under prices even as the Fed tightens.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

topic|HAL|https://www.outlookbusiness.co.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed flow. GE shipped four F404-IN20 engines in September, taking HAL's total to 14 and easing the Tejas Mk1A bottleneck. Honeywell expects to deliver 16 TPE331-12B engines for the HTT-40 ahead of the mid-2029 schedule. Against that, a retired Air Marshal says HAL has lost the IAF's faith, and the IAF still flies about 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned 42.5.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "GE ships four more F404 engines to HAL for Tejas Mk1A",
      "essay": "GE Aerospace shipped four F404-IN20 engines to HAL in September 2026, taking total deliveries to 14. GE sent three in August after handing over six by the end of the 2025-26 financial year, and expects to deliver eight more by the end of 2026.\n\nThe engines come from a $716 million contract signed in 2021 for 99 units, enough to power the first batch of 83 Tejas Mk1A jets ordered by the IAF. GE plans to raise its delivery rate to roughly 30 engines a year from next year and clear the backlog by 2028. HAL has built about 30 Mk1A airframes that are complete or nearly complete but waiting on engines.\n\nFor HAL, the schedule matters because the Tejas Mk1A is meant to replace retired aircraft. The IAF operates around 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned strength of 42.5. If GE holds the pace, HAL can fit its parked airframes, run flight tests and start handing over combat-ready jets in the coming months.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Honeywell expects HTT-40 engine deliveries ahead of schedule",
      "essay": "Honeywell Aerospace expects to deliver all 16 TPE331-12B engines for HAL's HTT-40 basic trainer ahead of the original mid-2029 deadline. Four engines are already delivered, with four more due by the end of 2026, taking the total to eight, and deliveries rising through 2027 and 2028.\n\nThe engines sit under a contract signed in July 2022 worth more than $100 million, covering 88 engines and kits plus maintenance and support. Honeywell supplies the first 16 directly, after which technology transfer lets HAL assemble the engines in India and expand its repair and overhaul work. Honeywell worked through supply-chain problems after a fire destroyed a supplier's facility, adding alternative sources and placing its own staff at supplier sites.\n\nHoneywell is also pitching the TPE331 family for India's unmanned aircraft and discussing navigation, electronic warfare and autonomy work with Indian firms, including startups. It has around 3,600 employees in India, nearly 3,000 of them engineers, and has invested more than $150 million over three decades in local infrastructure.",
      "sources": [1, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retired Air Marshal says HAL has lost the IAF's faith",
      "essay": "Air Marshal Choudhury (R) said HAL has lost the IAF's faith, pointing to the Tejas programme's delays. The criticism lands as the IAF works with around 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned 42.5, after the MiG-21 retirement.\n\nThe Tejas first flew on January 4, 2001. Twenty-five years on, the IAF has not re-equipped the squadrons losing MiG-21s at the pace needed. HAL has missed manufacturing timelines and blames the IAF for changing Air Staff Qualitative Requirements, the design benchmarks, while the IAF blames HAL. The article argues HAL's lines cannot build the 36-40 combat aircraft a year the Air Chief wants, and that new private production lines are the way out.\n\nFor HAL, the pressure is on execution, not orders. The IAF's own leadership pipeline, drawn largely from fighter ranks, also draws scrutiny in the piece.",
      "sources": [2, 4]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Infoedge/Naukri|https://www.saasri.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed Q2 update. Recruitment billings rose 12.6% to ₹613.8 crore, with underlying growth of 14-15% once renewal timing is stripped out. 99acres accelerated to 21.2% growth and matchmaking rose 23.1%. But Shiksha fell 27.2% as AI search cuts referral traffic, and the stock slipped 3%. Growth is steady, not accelerating.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Info Edge Q2 billings up 12.8%, recruitment grows 12.6%",
      "essay": "Info Edge reported standalone billings of ₹822.3 crore for Q2 FY27, up 12.8% from ₹729 crore a year earlier. For the first half, billings rose 13.5% to ₹1,559.3 crore.\n\nRecruitment, the largest segment and home to Naukri, grew 12.6% to ₹613.8 crore. Management said underlying growth was closer to 14-15% once it adjusted for the timing of customer renewals, which can shift reported figures between quarters. Adoption of its B2B AI products, AI-Rex and Talent Pulse, kept rising in both new sign-ups and revenue contribution.\n\nThe market still marked the stock down 3%. Reported growth sits below the 30%+ ARR expansion of high-growth pure-play SaaS firms, and the figures are unaudited, ahead of formal results. For a holder, the read is steady rather than accelerating: the core recruitment engine is fine, but it is not yet showing the AI-driven step-up in growth that would justify a higher multiple.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "99acres growth accelerates to 21.2%, matchmaking up 23.1%",
      "essay": "The real estate platform 99acres grew billings 21.2% year-on-year to ₹148.3 crore, up from 16.5% growth in Q1 FY27. For H1, 99acres billings rose 19.2% to ₹258.4 crore. The company said it held its share of category traffic, with web time-share at 49% and app time-share at 59% in July and August 2026, citing SimilarWeb.\n\nMatchmaking also grew. Jeevansathi billings rose 18.4% to ₹39.7 crore, and Aisle jumped 40%. Combined, the matchmaking vertical was up 23.1%.\n\nThese two segments are now the faster-growing parts of the portfolio, and they are pulling the blended growth rate up while recruitment plods along at low double digits. The traffic-share numbers matter because they suggest 99acres is not losing ground to rivals even as it raises prices. For a holder, this is the part of the update that supports the case for owning the stock.",
      "sources": [2, 3, 4, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shiksha billings fall 27.2% as AI search cuts traffic",
      "essay": "Shiksha, the education platform, saw billings drop 27.2% year-on-year to ₹20.5 crore from ₹28.1 crore. For H1, billings fell 24.5% to ₹55.1 crore.\n\nInfo Edge blamed the decline on AI-powered search tools that answer user queries directly, cutting the referral traffic that used to reach Shiksha from search engines. The company said it is adapting the business model and adding new offerings to reduce dependence on search, but expects billings growth to stay under pressure in the interim, with the transition playing out over the medium term.\n\nShiksha is small, so the direct hit to group billings is limited. The signal is the bigger issue: a search-dependent vertical can lose a quarter of its billings in a year when AI search intercepts the traffic. That is a warning for any part of the portfolio that relies on organic search rather than a logged-in, habitual user base.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Jio Platforms|https://www.tradingv.text:
{
  "stance": "bullish",
  "view": "Jio's IPO is on track with a possible ₹11-12 lakh crore valuation and a ₹37,700 crore issue size. Akash Ambani calls it a 'great responsibility' and hints at sharing value with retail investors. Jio will stay the most affordable operator even as Airtel raises postpaid tariffs by ₹50. The listing could also narrow Reliance's holding-company discount, which now sits at 36%.",
  "stories": [
    {
    "headline": "Jio IPO nears with ₹11-12 lakh crore valuation target",
    "essay": "Jio Platforms is moving toward what could be India's largest-ever IPO, with a proposed valuation of ₹11 lakh crore ($114 billion) and a possible issue size of ₹37,700 crore. The company filed its draft prospectus in June and received the markets regulator's final observations on August 28. Akash Ambani, Chairman of Reliance Jio and MD of Jio Platforms, told CNBC-TV18 and ET that the listing is a 'great responsibility' and the 'biggest moment' for the company, with the goal of sharing value with Indian citizens, including retail investors. He said the valuation and investor details would be known at the right time. The IPO could open as soon as October 21, according to reports. Motilal Oswal noted the offering could re-rate not just Reliance Industries but also Bharti Airtel. For Jio, the listing marks the start of its next growth phase, with a focus on an indigenous technology stack and affordable solutions for India's billion-plus users.",
    "sources": [1, 3, 5]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Jio stays affordable as Airtel raises postpaid tariffs by ₹50",
    "essay": "Bharti Airtel raised its postpaid tariffs across the board by ₹50, effective October 8, 2026, with existing subscribers moved to the nearest higher plan within a month. The hike translates to a 3-11% increase. Analysts expect telecom operators to raise tariffs by 12-15% over the next 4-5 months. Akash Ambani said Jio will always remain the most affordable player while delivering quality service. Jio's average revenue per user (ARPU) rose 3.3% year-on-year to ₹215.6 in the second quarter of the current financial year, while Airtel's ARPU increased 5.6% to ₹264. Ambani also said Jio is becoming a technology creator, not just a buyer, showcasing its own end-to-end satellite stack at India Mobile Congress. Jio is building a constellation of over 1,600 low-Earth orbit satellites with 23 ground stations to compete with Starlink, which has over 11,000 satellites. For Jio, the tariff gap with Airtel may pressure ARPU in the near term, but the affordability stance could help retain and grow its 50 crore subscriber base.",
    "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Reliance trades at 36% discount to Jio stake value",
    "essay": "Reliance Industries shares imply a roughly 36% discount to the value of its two-thirds stake in Jio Platforms, according to Nimish Maheshwari of Beat The Street. He sees about 25% as a sustainable gap after Jio lists, in line with how the market values Bharti Airtel's holdings in its listed subsidiaries. Jio may seek a valuation of about ₹11 trillion ($114 billion) in its IPO, which would value Reliance's stake at about ₹7.3 trillion, or 45% of the parent's current market value. Brokerages including Motilal Oswal, Yes Securities and Nuvama value the stake at ₹331 to 450 per Reliance share, implying it accounts for 27% to 37% of the parent's share price. Reliance shares have fallen 23% this year, compared with a 14% decline in the Nifty 50. Thea Jamison of Change Global Investment said how Reliance monetizes its Jio stake could be a significant source of value creation. For Jio, the listing will provide transparent price discovery, which could help narrow the holding-company discount and re-rate the parent.",
    "sources": [6]
    }
  ]
