      "sources": [1, 2, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Minutes hires Dibyendu Sen for brand partnerships",
      "essay": "Dibyendu Sen has joined Flipkart Minutes as associate director for brand partnerships and monetisation. He announced the move on LinkedIn. Sen comes from Criteo, where he spent over two years and most recently was senior manager for enterprise sales, working on retail media monetisation and advertising across Blinkit, Swiggy Instamart and Zepto. He previously spent over two years at Flipkart in senior manager roles covering FMCG, home and grocery ads, and nearly two years at Paytm Ads handling FMCG, D2C and F&B. He started in sales at Zee Entertainment Enterprises and later spent nearly six years at Star TV Network. The hire points to Flipkart building out advertising revenue inside its quick-commerce arm, a business where rival platforms already sell retail media.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Safari suitcase deals compared across Flipkart and Amazon",
      "essay": "Safari suitcases are heavily discounted on both Flipkart and Amazon, and the seller matters more than the discount percentage. Flipkart lists the Magnum Zeno and Magnum Fury medium suitcases at Rs 1,449 against a displayed MRP of Rs 9,370, and the Astra Neo set at Rs 3,799, or Rs 3,609 with an additional offer. Several of those listings are sold by SafariIndustriesLtd itself. On Amazon, a Safari Pentagon two-piece set was selling for Rs 2,989 against a displayed MRP of Rs 19,998, roughly 85% off, but that listing was fulfilled by Amazon and sold by RetailEZ Pvt Ltd. Safari's warranty covers manufacturing defects and excludes wear and tear, scratches, dents, zip damage and transit mishandling, and buyers must keep the original invoice. For Flipkart, the takeaway is that its marketplace still wins price checks when the brand sells directly on it.",
