topic|Flipkart|https://lbbonline.com/new.text:
{
  "stance": "bullish",
  "view": "Flipkart's Big Billion Days opened with 6.3 million concurrent users in a minute, 34% new customer growth, and strong premium demand. Large appliances rose 30% and tablets 73% year-on-year in the first hour. Flipkart Minutes orders jumped 3.5 times, showing quick commerce traction. The festive season is off to a strong start.",
  "stories": [
    {
    "headline": "Flipkart Big Billion Days opens with record 6.3 million concurrent users",
    "essay": "Flipkart's 13th Big Billion Days sale kicked off with 6.3 million concurrent users in a minute during the opening hour, with every 15th Indian online shopper visiting the platform. New customers grew 34 per cent, and Early Access generated over 287 million sessions. Nearly one in two visits came from Tier-III towns and beyond, with non-metro customers accounting for about 60 per cent of demand. Sardulgarh, Panskura, Sheikhpura and Kishanganj were among the fastest-growing markets.\n\nLarge appliance sales rose 30 per cent year-on-year in the first hour, while tablets grew 73 per cent. Beauty sales increased 42 per cent, led by men's grooming and self-care. One in four smartphone buyers opted for product exchange, and one in three chose no-cost EMI. Flipkart Minutes saw order volumes rise 3.5 times over regular days, with new customers up 11 times. An iPhone 17 was delivered in New Delhi in 3.4 minutes during Early Access.\n\nOnline festive sales are projected to grow as much as 29 per cent this year to up to ₹1.55 trillion, according to Datum Intelligence. Quick commerce is expected to account for about 16 per cent of festive sales, up from 12 per cent last year. The strong opening indicates robust consumer demand and Flipkart's ability to capture it across categories and geographies.",
    "sources": [4, 6]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Flipkart launches 'Ek Size Bada Le Lo' campaign for Big Billion Days TV deals",
    "essay": "Flipkart has launched a new campaign titled 'Ek Size Bada Le Lo' for its Big Billion Days sale, created by 22feet, part of Omnicom Advertising India. The campaign draws on the familiar Indian childhood experience of mothers advising children to buy clothes one size bigger, linking it to upgrading to a larger TV without a bigger bill during the sale.\n\nThe film uses humour and nostalgia to connect festive offers with a relatable cultural insight. Ishan Mehta, executive creative director at 22feet, said the insight of parents buying oversized clothes was relatable, and using that mantra for large-screen TVs was the creative unlock. The campaign will be amplified across digital platforms to reach consumers exploring Big Billion Days deals.\n\nThis marketing push supports Flipkart's efforts to drive big-ticket purchases during the festive season, complementing the strong sales momentum seen in the opening hours.",
    "sources": [1]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Flipkart discounts MacBook Neo amid memory crisis, offers bank and exchange deals",
    "essay": "Flipkart is offering discounts on the Apple MacBook Neo during its Big Billion Days Sale 2026, as a memory crisis pushes laptop prices higher. The MacBook Neo comes in two storage variants, with bank discounts of Rs.3,000 off the 256GB model via ICICI Bank credit card and Rs.3,500 off the 512GB model via HDFC Bank debit card. Exchange benefits go up to Rs.48,250 for the 256GB variant and Rs.58,650 for the 512GB model, depending on the old device and location.\n\nThe MacBook Neo weighs around 1.23kg and has a 13-inch Liquid Retina display, with Apple claiming up to 16 hours of battery life. Both variants have 8GB of unified memory, which may limit demanding workloads. The 512GB model costs Rs.10,000 more at the listed price but offers twice the storage.\n\nThe discounts aim to make the entry-level MacBook more affordable for students and office users. However, buyers should compare final prices and bank offers, as the memory crisis continues to pressure laptop pricing.",
    "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Bitcoin|https://www.coindesk.com/m.text:
{
  "stance": "bearish",
  "view": "The flow is bearish. US spot Bitcoin ETFs lost $729 million over two sessions as BTC returned to the average ETF holder's breakeven of $81,722, and long-term holder buying collapsed 96% to 1,051 BTC. A rebound to $82,500 came without fresh leverage, futures open interest down 1.9% to $27.1 billion, and $931 million of long liquidations show the bounce is fragile.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Bitcoin ETFs Shed $729 Million as Price Hits Breakeven",
      "essay": "US spot Bitcoin ETFs lost $729 million over two sessions this week, just as BTC returned to the funds' estimated average entry price. Bloomberg Intelligence analyst James Seyffart puts that average at $81,722 per coin. With Bitcoin at $81,607, the average ETF position is marginally underwater, and holders are treating breakeven as an exit door.\n\nThe headline total hides a split. BlackRock's IBIT remains a net buyer in October, absorbing $332.5 million, though it shed $207.7 million on Oct. 7, its largest withdrawal of the month. Fidelity's FBTC alone lost more than the entire market, with outflows in five of October's six trading sessions. Excluding IBIT, the remaining ETFs lost a combined $739.9 million. US spot Ethereum ETFs have logged eight straight sessions of outflows, losing $641.3 million since Sept. 29.\n\nThe pressure matters because spot ETFs were a major buyer during September's rally, absorbing more than $2.3 billion between Sept. 21 and Sept. 30. That demand now looks anchored to a price level rather than long-term conviction. The test is whether these funds can attract new buyers once breakeven no longer offers an easy exit.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Long-Term Holder Buying Drops 96% as Momentum Diverges",
      "essay": "Bitcoin trades near $82,300, still up about 28% over three months after this week's drop. A hidden bullish divergence on the daily chart suggests the uptrend can survive, but the buying that powered it has faded.\n\nBetween September 2 and October 8, Bitcoin made a higher low while the Relative Strength Index, a 0 to 100 measure of buying momentum, slid from about 65 to 47. That mix, a hidden bullish divergence, shows up inside uptrends and suggests a dip has cooled momentum without breaking the trend. Selling volume backs the read: red volume bars have grown since October 6, but the October 8 bar was still about 18% smaller than the October 2 peak.\n\nThe buyers explain the momentum drop. Hodler Net Position Change, a Glassnode metric tracking the monthly change in coins held by long-term holders, peaked at 25,734 BTC on September 20. By October 8 it had dropped 96% to 1,051 BTC, its weakest positive reading in three months. Holder buying faded like this in late July, and the metric stayed negative from August 2 to August 30 as long-term holders sold into August's rally. Bitcoin still rose 24% that month, with spot ETFs buying $3.04 billion during a nine-day inflow streak from August 17 to 27. This time, funds pulled $731 million on October 7 and 8.\n\nA daily close above $83,325 is the first step. Reclaiming $88,086 could draw holders back. A drop under $80,383 would break the higher low and cancel the divergence, putting $75,041 back in play.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bitcoin Steadies Near $82,500 After Trump Rules Out Iran Strike",
      "essay": "Bitcoin heads into the weekend about 4% lower than last Friday, even after recovering to around $82,500 from a low near $80,300 on Thursday. Ether slid 9% over the week to around $2,500.\n\nThe rebound followed a Truth Social post from President Donald Trump on Thursday saying the U.S. would not attack Iran before the Nov. 3 midterm elections. Brent crude slipped about 1% to around $103 a barrel. U.S. equity futures rose, with Nasdaq 100 futures up 0.83% and S&P 500 futures adding 0.44%. Smaller tokens led the recovery, with the CoinDesk 80 up 2.2% since midnight UTC, more than twice the gain in the CoinDesk 5.\n\nThe bounce has yet to undo Thursday's damage. The CoinDesk 100 is still 2.2% lower over 24 hours and DeFi tokens are down nearly 4%. U.S.-listed bitcoin, ether and zcash ETFs posted outflows on Thursday, leaving XRP funds as the only crypto products to take in money. Bitcoin futures open interest slipped 1.9% over 24 hours to $27.1 billion and has barely moved since Thursday's flush, suggesting the rebound came without fresh leverage. Funding rates remain positive at about 5% annualized, meaning longs still pay to hold. Accounts holding long bitcoin positions outnumber shorts by nearly two to one, with the aggregated long/short ratio at 1.85, or around 65% long. CoinGlass shows $1.09 billion of liquidations over the past 24 hours, with longs accounting for $931 million, or around 85% of the total.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Gold|https://fortune.com/article/c.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Gold recovered this week, closing near $4,187 an ounce after bouncing from $4,066. Falling consumer sentiment (46.3) and rising inflation expectations (4.7% year-ahead) are supportive. But the Fed minutes kept a December hike on the table, a firm dollar and high Treasury yields are headwinds, and next week's CPI is the real test. Rangebound until that number lands.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Gold bounces from $4,066 to near $4,200 on softer yields",
      "essay": "Gold closed the week at $4,187 an ounce, up more than 1% from the prior Friday, after a volatile stretch. The metal started the week at $4,142.40, fell as low as $4,066.31 on Wednesday when a stronger dollar and rate-hike expectations hit non-yielding assets, then rallied Thursday and Friday as the dollar softened, Treasury yields eased and oil pulled back on reports of productive U.S.-Iran talks. Spot gold hit a weekly high of $4,207.48 early Friday.\n\nThe catalyst for Friday's move was the University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey, which came in at 46.3 for October, below the 47.6 forecast and September's 48.1. Year-ahead inflation expectations rose to 4.7% from 4.6%, the highest since May, and long-run expectations went to 3.5% from 3.4%. Buyers keep showing up near $4,100, and gold-backed ETFs took inflows during the decline.\n\nThe ceiling is clear. The September Fed minutes showed most policymakers still expect a rate increase before year-end, and markets price a December hike. Fawad Razaqzada at FOREX.com said investors are looking past tighter policy because they worry inflation may overshoot on high oil prices. The next test is Wednesday's CPI.",
      "sources": [2, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gold holds $4,100 support but $4,200 caps the rally",
      "essay": "Analysts see gold stuck between $4,100 and $4,200. Ole Hansen at Saxo Bank expects the metal to stay rangebound next week, though he called the recovery from below $4,100 encouraging, and said he remains bullish over the medium term because high borrowing costs eventually force easier monetary policy. Lukman Otunuga at FXTM said the medium-to-longer-term outlook still favors bears, with markets overwhelmingly pricing a Fed hike by December 2026. He flagged Wednesday's CPI as the risk: sustained weakness below $4,200 opens the path to $4,100 and $4,000, while a breakout could target the 100-day SMA near $4,260 and $4,300.\n\nDavid Morrison at Trade Nation said the daily MACD is flattening and trying to curl up, but it has not reached the oversold levels seen in July, so upside momentum could run out fast. A stronger dollar adds pressure: Adam Turnquist at LPL Financial said the U.S. Dollar Index has broken above a double-bottom pattern and could climb to 107 points, and gold historically struggles in that environment because a stronger dollar makes it more expensive for foreign buyers.",
      "sources": [6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vietnam gold bars and rings rise 0.91% on global rally",
      "essay": "Saigon Gold Jewelry Company's gold bar price gained 0.91% to VND144.3 million (US$5,585.55) per tael on Friday, up nearly 0.6% for the week. Gold ring prices rose 0.91% to VND143.8 million per tael. A tael equals 37.5 grams, or 1.2 ounces.\n\nThe move tracked global gold, which rose more than 1% on Friday on a softer dollar, easing Treasury yields and lower oil prices. Spot gold rose 1.1% to $4,177.44 an ounce after hitting a two-month low on Wednesday. U.S. gold futures gained 1.1% to $4,202.00.\n\nTony Sage, CEO of Critical Metals, said possible tightening later on could keep gold at risk, and traders will watch upcoming economic data for policy clues ahead of the Fed's October meeting. Softer numbers or guidance could push yields down and support gold.",
