    {
      "headline": "Tejas and Qualcomm demo 6G sensing tech at India Mobile Congress",
      "essay": "Tejas Networks and Qualcomm Technologies jointly demonstrated Integrated Sensing and Communication (ISAC) at India Mobile Congress 2026. ISAC turns wireless networks into radar, using ordinary communication signals to detect and track objects like moving vehicles and drones without separate sensing gear.\n\nThe setup pairs Tejas Networks' 5G base station with Qualcomm's Radio Unit and Distributed Unit chipsets, plus low-power compute and AI inference. The companies say it shows existing 5G infrastructure can add drone and vehicle tracking, traffic management and critical-infrastructure monitoring, and points toward the AI-native architecture planned for 6G. Tejas CTO Kumar N. Sivarajan said ISAC could let mobile operators sell spatial intelligence, not just connectivity. Qualcomm India president Rajen Vagadia called it a step in India's 6G journey.\n\nFor Tejas, this is a technology showcase with a major chip partner, not a commercial order. No customers, volumes or financial terms were disclosed. It keeps Tejas visible in the 6G conversation, but the revenue path is still years out.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
